1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
In this lesson, you're going to bring together the healthcare research agent,

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.873
insurance policy agent, and healthcare provider agent

3
00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.476
using the open-source BeeAI Framework

4
00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:11.478
created by IBM research.

5
00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:16.016
This framework dynamically calls agents when needed, creating a multi-agent system.

6
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:19.419
Each agent is built with different underlying technologies.

7
00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.088
But since you've already wrapped them as A2A servers,

8
00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.791
you can orchestrate them as part of the same team.

9
00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:27.594
That way they communicate using the same protocol.

10
00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:29.963
Kind of akin to speaking the same

11
00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:31.898
language, even if they have different accents.

12
00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:34.501
Let's go. The open source BeeAI framework

13
00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.370
is a developer tool for building production-ready

14
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.540
AI agents with an emphasis on agent reliability.

15
00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.143
BeeAI goes beyond simple prompting

16
00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:47.647
by providing a lightweight yet powerful approach to reliable agent development.

17
00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.850
With built-in constraint enforcement and rule-based governance

18
00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:55.155
that preserves reasoning abilities while ensuring predictable behavior.

19
00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.258
It's not enough to create an agent. It's important that you are

20
00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:00.060
confident that the agent won't act outside

21
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.095
of the constraints that you set for it.

22
00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.297
As we walk through the notebook, we'll

23
00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:06.499
learn about the features of the BeeAI Framework,

24
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.103
like its ability to enforce rules that drive agent behavior,

25
00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:13.406
Event Driven Middleware, pluggable observability,

26
00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:17.811
built-in memory management, and MCP and A2A nativeness.

27
00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:21.247
Let's get started. We'll start by importing your dependencies.

28
00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:25.718
You'll see we're importing asyncio for handling asynchronous operations

29
00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:29.122
in a notebook environment, the standard os module,

30
00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.524
and some display utilities from IPython.

31
00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:35.829
We're also importing a helper function called authenticate from our helpers module,

32
00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.631
so we can use VertexAI as

33
00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.834
our inference provider for the orchestrator agent.

34
00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.837
Next, we need to ensure that all of our individual agents

35
00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:46.239
that we created in the previous lessons

36
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:48.241
are running in their own terminal,

37
00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:50.543
so that when we build the orchestrator agent,

38
00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:52.545
they can be found and responsive.

39
00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.582
Let's start the policy agent that you built in lesson four.

40
00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.885
It's already running because it's been ran in a previous lesson.

41
00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.688
Then we'll start the research agent from lesson six.

42
00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.692
And finally, the provider agent from lesson eight.

43
00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.896
All right, now let's get to the exciting part.

44
00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.632
I'm going to create what's called a requirement agent

45
00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:16.636
to dynamically orchestrate and call the other agents when needed.

46
00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:19.339
It's called the requirement agent

47
00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.040
because you can ensure that it

48
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.009
enforces certain rules by setting conditional requirements.

49
00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.311
But you'll learn more about that in a few minutes.

50
00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:28.414
For now, let's ensure that you have the imports installed

51
00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.617
and helper function for observability.

52
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:34.654
I use the beeai_framework adapters to import the needed modules,

53
00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.389
which make it simple to wrap

54
00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.458
a BeeAI agent as an A2A server.

55
00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:41.027
The VertexAIChatModel to use vertexai as the

56
00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.630
inference provider for the main orchestrator agent.

57
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.033
BeeAI also has 15 plus built-in providers

58
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.436
and can be easily extended to add custom inference providers.

59
00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:54.641
And finally, the remaining modules that add memory, ConditionalRequirement,

60
00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:59.412
the HandoffTool and the ThinkTool, which we'll cover as we implement.

61
00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:03.082
Now we load the environment variables and authenticate to Google Cloud,

62
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:06.586
since we're using Vertex as the LLM inference provider.

63
00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:10.290
Then we set up the connection details and get the ports

64
00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.292
for where each of the agents is running.

65
00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.994
And I define a port for the new healthcare agent

66
00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:18.298
that you'll be building in this lesson using the RequirementAgent.

67
00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:21.935
Next, I use the abstraction in the BeeAI framework

68
00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:25.939
to make an A2A agent instance of the running servers.

69
00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:29.842
First, I create the A2A object pointing to that agent's URL.

70
00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:32.245
This acts as the A2A client

71
00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.747
that will take care of forwarding the request

72
00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.549
to the server agent through A2A.

73
00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:38.751
Then I give it UnconstrainedMemory

74
00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.654
so it can manage conversation history. The BeeAI framework

75
00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.924
also has other built-in memory optimization strategies,

76
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:48.528
like sliding window, which keeps only the most recent K entries,

77
00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:53.132
token memory, which manages token usage to stay within model context limits,

78
00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:56.836
and summarize memory, which maintains a single summarization of the conversation.

79
00:03:56.836 --> 00:04:01.641
Determining your memory strategy makes a large impact on agent performance

80
00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:03.443
and is something that you should

81
00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:05.545
experiment with as you build agentic systems.

82
00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.715
Finally, I call check_agent_exists method,

83
00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:12.518
which reaches out to the agent server and fetches its AgentCard,

84
00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:15.221
which is like the agent's metadata profile.

85
00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:18.925
It includes the agent's name, description, and capabilities.

86
00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.994
See how after I run it,

87
00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:23.029
it confirms that the agent has been initialized?

88
00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:27.233
The check_agent_exists method validated that each server is running

89
00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:29.936
and retrieve their AgentCard information.

90
00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.106
This AgentCard data will be crucial in the next session

91
00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.808
when I set up an orchestrator. It tells

92
00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.511
the requirement agent what each specialized agent can do.

93
00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:41.014
Now let's do the same for the research_agent.

94
00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:44.584
And finally, for the provider agent.

95
00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:46.986
Now all these agents are ready

96
00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.988
to be called by the Orchestrator agent.

97
00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:52.992
It's time to create the brain of the operation, the Orchestrator agent,

98
00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:55.395
which I'm calling the healthcare agent.

99
00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.563
But before I dive into the code,

100
00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.599
let's talk about what makes the agent special

101
00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:02.302
and why it's important for production AI systems.

102
00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:06.205
Traditional AI agents exhibit unpredictable behavior.

103
00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:09.409
You might have an agent that works perfectly in testing,

104
00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.111
but in production it starts skipping critical steps,

105
00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:15.915
using wrong tools, or terminating before completing its task.

106
00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:18.518
Different language models also produce

107
00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:21.321
wildly different execution patterns for the same task.

108
00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:24.824
What works with one model might completely fail with another.

109
00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:28.461
This unpredictability is actually one of the biggest barriers

110
00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:31.264
to deploying AI agents in real world applications

111
00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.666
where reliability matters.

112
00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:37.570
The Requirement Agent solves this through declarative rule-based constraints

113
00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.939
called Conditional Requirements.

114
00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:42.442
Instead of hoping your agent will do the right thing

115
00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:44.744
and put directions in your system prompt,

116
00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:46.746
which is not enforceable,

117
00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.748
you can explicitly declare rules

118
00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:51.451
about when and how tools should be used.

119
00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.887
The framework then enforces these rules

120
00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.090
regardless of which language model you're using underneath.

121
00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.394
It works by checking the conditional rules before each LLM invocation.

122
00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.563
Only the tools whose Conditional Requirements

123
00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:05.698
are satisfied at that point of time

124
00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:08.701
are exposed to the LLM as callable options.

125
00:06:09.102 --> 00:06:11.904
For example, if an agent has 10 tools,

126
00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:14.407
but in the third turn of the conversation,

127
00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:16.809
only four of the 10 tools should be used

128
00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:18.478
according to the logic that you set.

129
00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:20.446
in the conditional requirements,

130
00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:22.348
then only the four tools are exposed

131
00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.250
to the LLM as options to call.

132
00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.254
This enforces the rules at the framework level

133
00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:31.958
rather than suggesting them in the system prompt, which is unenforceable.

134
00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:34.460
Limits unnecessary context bloat,

135
00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.963
which affects performance and production costs,

136
00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:40.466
and reduces the necessary reasoning ability of the model

137
00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:42.668
to choose the correct tool at the correct time.

138
00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:44.670
Now let's configure the Requirement Agent.

139
00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:47.807
First, we give it a name and a description.

140
00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:52.111
Healthcare agent is our concierge that's customized to your policy.

141
00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:54.847
This metadata will be important when we

142
00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:57.517
expose this agent as an A2A server itself.

143
00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.519
Next, the LLM.

144
00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:03.322
We're using Vertex AI's gemini-2.5-flash model.

145
00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:06.025
Notice we're enabling parallel tool calls.

146
00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:10.129
This means the agent can call multiple specialized agents simultaneously if needed.

147
00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:14.100
For example, if a user asks about both insurance coverage and

148
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:18.137
a nearby provider, it can query both agents at the same time.

149
00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:20.306
Now let's look at the tools array.

150
00:07:20.606 --> 00:07:24.410
We have four tools, the think tool. This is a reasoning tool.

151
00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.646
I can change the conditional requirements on

152
00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:29.115
the think tool to enable different reasoning patterns.

153
00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:31.617
For instance, I can create a

154
00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:34.120
react or reasoning and then acting pattern

155
00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.456
by forcing the think tool to be

156
00:07:36.456 --> 00:07:38.524
run in between every other tool call.

157
00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:42.895
or a planning pattern by forcing the ThinkTool at just step one.

158
00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:45.198
I also have three HandoffTools.

159
00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:47.266
One for each specialized agent.

160
00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.269
The HandoffTool is similar to a normal tool,

161
00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.372
except it doesn't just execute the tool as an isolated function

162
00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:55.374
and return the result to the main agent.

163
00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:57.944
It also passes the state and context off to

164
00:07:57.944 --> 00:08:00.680
the other agent the task is being handed off to.

165
00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.882
The abstraction makes it easy to

166
00:08:02.882 --> 00:08:05.084
call agents as tools without needing to

167
00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.086
implement the handoff logic yourself.

168
00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:10.056
Notice that we're using the agent_card.description

169
00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:12.859
from each A2A agent as the tool description.

170
00:08:13.059 --> 00:08:16.863
Remember how we fetched the agent cards earlier with check_agent_exists?

171
00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:20.266
That's why. The descriptions tell the requirement agent

172
00:08:20.466 --> 00:08:22.869
exactly what each specialized agent can do.

173
00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:26.272
Next, we get to the differentiator for the BeeAI framework.

174
00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.274
The ConditionalRequirement.

175
00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.077
Because we want the LLM's reasoning ability to

176
00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:34.046
dynamically select the correct agent

177
00:08:34.046 --> 00:08:36.249
to hand off tasks to at the correct time.

178
00:08:36.249 --> 00:08:38.251
We won't force a certain order

179
00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:40.253
or necessarily even which agent should be called.

180
00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:42.255
But we can put some rules around how

181
00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:44.056
the agent should behave if they are called.

182
00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:47.059
In this case, we're only setting one ConditionalRequirement,

183
00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:49.061
but I'll speak about a couple others.

184
00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:51.764
We want to force the agent to think at the first turn

185
00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:56.068
to do some planning. And then after every additional tool call,

186
00:08:56.068 --> 00:09:00.840
but not consecutively. This enforces a thinking and then a ReAct pattern.

187
00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.443
We can also add additional conditional requirements

188
00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:07.446
on our agents themselves that are being handed off to.

189
00:09:07.747 --> 00:09:11.751
In this case it's commented out, but you can add conditional requirements

190
00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:15.454
on each of the agent tools that say max consecutive allowed,

191
00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:18.658
minimum consecutive allowed, or even

192
00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:22.261
which agent should be called at which step of the agent process.

193
00:09:22.929 --> 00:09:25.031
many built-in parameters to choose from

194
00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:27.033
when setting your ConditionalRequirement logic,

195
00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:30.236
such as minimum or maximum invocations of a tool allowed,

196
00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:34.440
forcing certain tools only before or after other tools have been called,

197
00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.742
or even your own custom conditions.

198
00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:38.911
You can see the entire parameter reference

199
00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:40.613
and how to build custom ones in

200
00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:42.515
the resources at the bottom of the notebook.

201
00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:44.784
Now we can set our agent's role.

202
00:09:44.784 --> 00:09:46.886
which is a Healthcare Concierge

203
00:09:46.986 --> 00:09:49.088
and the instructions that it should follow.

204
00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:51.057
Note that we don't need to repeat

205
00:09:51.057 --> 00:09:52.992
the rules that we put in the ConditionalRequirement

206
00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:54.794
in natural language in the system prompt

207
00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:58.664
because the conditional requirements give us an enforceable way

208
00:09:58.664 --> 00:10:00.866
to ensure that the agent follows directions,

209
00:10:00.866 --> 00:10:03.169
rather than a suggestion in the system prompt.

210
00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:06.839
Instead, we can keep our system prompt more concise and clean

211
00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:10.142
without overburdening the LLM's reasoning abilities.

212
00:10:10.676 --> 00:10:13.179
Okay, now it's time to run the full workflow.

213
00:10:13.179 --> 00:10:15.681
In the response variable, let's wait

214
00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:18.184
until the agent runs and ask it,

215
00:10:18.184 --> 00:10:20.486
I'm based in Austin, Texas.

216
00:10:20.486 --> 00:10:22.688
How do I get the mental health therapy near me

217
00:10:22.688 --> 00:10:24.690
and what does my insurance cover?

218
00:10:24.924 --> 00:10:28.127
Now because we want to see what's happening under the hood,

219
00:10:28.427 --> 00:10:30.730
we can use the BeeAI framework's middleware

220
00:10:30.896 --> 00:10:33.799
to view the agent trace as a form of observability.

221
00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.968
The middleware can also be used as a

222
00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:37.803
way to hook into the agent emitted events,

223
00:10:38.004 --> 00:10:42.008
and inject things like content safety filters or prompt injection detection.

224
00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:44.010
You can check out more examples on

225
00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.711
how to use middleware in the documentation.

226
00:10:45.911 --> 00:10:49.482
So finally, let's display the agent's last response,

227
00:10:49.582 --> 00:10:51.283
which is its final answer.

228
00:10:51.484 --> 00:10:53.486
We can see that the agent has started to work

229
00:10:53.586 --> 00:10:56.389
and it's going through each of those ConditionalRequirement

230
00:10:56.489 --> 00:10:58.791
to check for what tools it's allowed to call.

231
00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:00.993
Then it calls the VertexAIChatModel.

232
00:11:01.293 --> 00:11:03.195
It's decided to call a ThinkTool

233
00:11:03.295 --> 00:11:04.997
because we've forced that tool at step one.

234
00:11:05.097 --> 00:11:07.600
And it will go through the process over and over again

235
00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.602
until it has a final answer.

236
00:11:10.803 --> 00:11:12.805
It's produced its final answer.

237
00:11:12.805 --> 00:11:14.473
So we can see that it gave

238
00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:16.375
us a summary of mental health therapy options

239
00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:19.245
near you and the insurance coverage.

240
00:11:19.478 --> 00:11:21.347
So it pulled from the provider agent,

241
00:11:21.547 --> 00:11:24.383
which provider that we can see within our insurance plan.

242
00:11:24.884 --> 00:11:27.186
And then from the insurance policy agent,

243
00:11:27.186 --> 00:11:30.656
some information about outpatient services, inpatient services,

244
00:11:30.656 --> 00:11:33.359
and important notes on our individual policy.

245
00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:35.861
Notice how it didn't call information from the research agent.

246
00:11:36.896 --> 00:11:39.165
That's because we didn't explicitly say that

247
00:11:39.165 --> 00:11:41.400
the research agent needed to be called

248
00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.102
and the orchestrator didn't deem that it

249
00:11:43.102 --> 00:11:44.804
needed to be called for this circumstance.

250
00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:47.907
If we wanted to enforce the research agent also be called,

251
00:11:47.907 --> 00:11:51.711
we could add a conditional requirement that had a minimum invocation

252
00:11:51.711 --> 00:11:53.713
or a maximum invocation for how many

253
00:11:53.713 --> 00:11:55.715
times the research agent could be called.

254
00:11:55.915 --> 00:11:58.184
In order to be able to run the healthcare

255
00:11:58.184 --> 00:12:00.486
agent as an A2A agent on its own server,

256
00:12:00.686 --> 00:12:02.988
we need to write the code to a Python file.

257
00:12:02.988 --> 00:12:04.523
This code helps us do that.

258
00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:07.560
It's the same logic that we just created above,

259
00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.529
but formatted as classes and functions.

260
00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:13.532
Rather than being ran in a Jupyter notebook cell.

261
00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:26.779
Now that we have the healthcare agent

262
00:12:26.779 --> 00:12:28.547
code written out to its own file,

263
00:12:28.814 --> 00:12:30.449
the only change we need to make

264
00:12:30.449 --> 00:12:32.351
to run the agent as an A2A agent

265
00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:36.122
is to add a single A2A server registration statement.

266
00:12:36.455 --> 00:12:38.524
The BeeAI framework makes it easy

267
00:12:38.724 --> 00:12:41.127
to turn any agent into an A2A agent

268
00:12:41.427 --> 00:12:44.130
by providing its A2A server abstraction.

269
00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:48.934
We set our config by assigning the port, protocol, and host.

270
00:12:49.135 --> 00:12:51.570
We add memory, which is the session

271
00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.340
memory, rather than the individual agent context memory.

272
00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.642
and then we register the agent and serve it.

273
00:12:57.243 --> 00:13:00.646
We're appending this to the same a2a_healthcare_agent.py file.

274
00:13:00.946 --> 00:13:02.782
But I wanted to separate it out for

275
00:13:02.782 --> 00:13:04.617
you to see just how simple the actual

276
00:13:04.683 --> 00:13:07.153
process of creating the A2A server is.

277
00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:10.256
Now let's start the BeeAI healthcare agent as

278
00:13:10.256 --> 00:13:12.658
its own A2A server in a new terminal.

279
00:13:13.159 --> 00:13:17.163
This demonstrates that the A2A agent that calls other A2A agents

280
00:13:17.463 --> 00:13:20.166
can itself be exposed as an A2A agent.

281
00:13:20.666 --> 00:13:23.469
Let's type uv run

282
00:13:23.836 --> 00:13:28.836
a2a_healthcare_agent.py

283
00:13:30.242 --> 00:13:31.844
to start the server.

284
00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:34.547
Now we can see that the server has started

285
00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:36.782
and all of the other agents that are

286
00:13:36.782 --> 00:13:38.918
being used as handoff tools have been initialized.

287
00:13:39.318 --> 00:13:42.321
Now let's create the client that calls the healthcare agent

288
00:13:42.421 --> 00:13:46.525
running on the A2A server. We use our A2AAgent wrapper

289
00:13:46.625 --> 00:13:49.128
and point it to the URL that the agent is running on.

290
00:13:49.728 --> 00:13:52.031
Then we add memory for the agent itself.

291
00:13:52.331 --> 00:13:55.534
We capture the agent run response in the responses variable,

292
00:13:55.835 --> 00:13:58.537
and just like we did before, we ask the same question

293
00:13:58.838 --> 00:14:02.041
and display the trajectory using middleware for observability

294
00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:08.113
and voila. The A2A agent client is invoking

295
00:14:08.113 --> 00:14:11.217
an A2A agent in a server that calls other A2A agents

296
00:14:11.217 --> 00:14:15.054
on separate servers. And there we have it.

297
00:14:15.354 --> 00:14:17.523
In this lesson, we used the

298
00:14:17.523 --> 00:14:20.059
open source BeeAI framework by IBM Research

299
00:14:20.059 --> 00:14:22.361
to create an A2A agent

300
00:14:22.361 --> 00:14:27.333
that dynamically calls other A2A agents written using different frameworks.

301
00:14:27.633 --> 00:14:31.737
And then we made the main A2A agent an A2A server itself.

302
00:14:32.037 --> 00:14:34.740
We hope that you saw the power of conditional requirements

303
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:39.044
that make it easy to set rule-based constraints on agent behavior

304
00:14:39.345 --> 00:14:43.315
and ensure that your agent acts within their allowed scope every time.
